/**
 * Every function touches the process or the machine and is declared with effects. `sleep` and
 * `exit` are also built in: they need no import. The terminal is `io`'s.
 */

// The declarations of the operators of the C++ module `modules/process` (design principles 6.4).
// This file is the one source of their signatures; the C++ side
// keeps the kernels, the executor, the purity and the resources: `sleep` waits on the clock,
// `whoami` and `cpu_count` read the environment, and `thread_count` reads the process (design
// principles 3.3). `exit` is the built-in operator of `src/builtin/modules/globals.cpp`, not a
// group of this module, so it is not declared here.
//
// No signature of this module is computed from an argument: `sleep` answers nothing and the other
// three answer `string` and `int` whatever the values are. `modules/process` therefore exports no
// operator group of its own, and the module needs no `stdlib/signatures/process.cml`.

module process

/// Suspends the program for the given number of milliseconds.
/// @effect waits
/// @fails fault `InvalidArgument` when the duration is negative
/// @example sleep(100)
export func sleep(milliseconds: int): void

/// The name of the user the process runs as.
/// @effect reads the environment
/// @fails fault `RuntimeError` when the name cannot be found
/// @example whoami()    // 'alice'
export func whoami(): string

/// The number of hardware threads, at least 1.
/// @effect reads the machine
/// @example cpu_count()    // 8
export func cpu_count(): int

/// The number of threads this process has, the caller included: the count the operating system
/// reports for the process, not the number of processors. A test reads it to pin how many worker
/// threads a run creates.
/// @effect reads the process
/// @example thread_count()    // 1 at -t 1
export func thread_count(): int